        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Écarté',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Ã‰cartÃ© (French for 'discarded') is one of the most elegant and historically significant two-player trick-taking games from France. Popularized in the early 19th century, it is famous for its unique card ranking (where the King is highest and the Ace sits between the King and Queen) and its signature 'proposal' mechanic, where players must negotiate whether to discard and draw new cards. It is a game of high tension, as refusing a proposal can lead to double penalties, making every decision a psychological contest.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Ã‰cartÃ© evolved from the older game 'Triomphe' and emerged in its modern form during the Napoleonic era. It was the premier gambling and social game for the European aristocracy throughout the 1800s.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Écarté is played by exactly two players. The players cut to determine the dealer; the elder hand (non-dealer) sits opposite the dealer. There are no partnerships.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32ndcard French pack (A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32ndcard French pack (A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '1. Five cards are dealt to each player (usually in sets of 2 and 3).\n2. The 11th card is turned face-up to determine the Trump Suit.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
